    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-319842",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point",
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{2}-4x+7){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}-4x+7$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+7)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0-0+3)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "3"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "3"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-319842",
        "kp_title": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-319829",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point",
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "0"
            },
