    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-48993",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point",
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=xln⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=ln⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(ln⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+ln⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=e} & =1+ln⁡x \\\\  & =1+ln⁡e \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
